# Git LFS tracking.
#
# Scope, as measured on 2026-10-21: of the 239 tracked files these rules used
# to cover, exactly ONE is stored as an LFS object today (`3DScifiStarterKitFree
# .zip`). The other 238 are ordinary blobs, because the migration that was
# supposed to move them never ran. That gap was not cosmetic — see below — so
# the rules have been narrowed to what is actually in effect, and the rest is
# recorded as intent rather than pretence.
#
# Why the gap was dangerous, not just inaccurate:
#   * any machine WITH git-lfs sees all 238 files as permanently modified,
#     because clean(real file) = pointer != the raw blob in HEAD. CI is such a
#     machine, and a dirty worktree is why the web-preview job's payload commit
#     could not rebase or push (measured: three retries, then failure);
#   * the next `git add` of one of those files would have replaced a real
#     35 MB of evidence images with a 133-byte pointer. A plain clone would
#     then be missing the files and nobody local would notice, because
#     without git-lfs the filter is inert and everything looks fine.
#
# Rules of thumb now written down rather than assumed:
#   * anything the game or the preview loads from a plain `git clone` must NOT
#     be LFS-tracked (`web/index.pck`, `web/index.wasm`,
#     `engine/web_templates/*.zip`, `game/assets/**`, fonts, videos, sources);
#   * everything else may be tracked, but only once the blobs have actually
#     been migrated — otherwise the attributes lie, and the lie is what bites.
#
# Setup on a machine that has git-lfs:
#     scripts/setup_lfs.sh            # verify + report (no history rewrite)
#     scripts/setup_lfs.sh --migrate  # rewrite history (destructive, off by default)

# Third-party archives. `3DScifiStarterKitFree.zip` is the one file these
# rules genuinely apply to.
*.zip filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text

# The web export templates are NOT under LFS, even though they are zips: they
# exist so a local `godot --export-release` needs no 1.2 GB template download,
# and a pointer is exactly what makes that impossible. All four are committed
# as 133-byte pointers today; the web-preview job copies the real archives in,
# so the first run that gets to push will replace them.
engine/web_templates/*.zip -filter -diff -merge

# The web preview payload is deliberately NOT under LFS.
#
# It was, once, and it broke the thing it existed for: `web/index.wasm` and
# `web/index.pck` are what the browser loads, and a plain `git clone` fetches
# LFS *pointers* instead (133 bytes of text), so the preview died at
# `CompileError: expected magic word 00 61 73 6d`. Two things make pointers
# fatal here rather than merely inconvenient:
#   * CI checks out with actions/checkout@v4 and no `lfs: true`, so the job
#     that runs tools/web_qa.mjs against web/index.html got the pointer too;
#   * there is no git-lfs in this sandbox at all, and with `filter=lfs` set,
#     `git add` on these files fails outright ("external filter 'git-lfs
#     clean' failed").
# Yes, the pack is 35 MB and CI regenerates it. It stays in the tree because
# the preview has to work from a bare clone; if the churn ever matters the
# fix is to stop committing it and publish the CI artifact, not to hide it
# behind a filter that makes the repository un-runnable.

# INTENDED for LFS, not yet migrated — do not re-add these lines until
# `scripts/setup_lfs.sh --migrate` has actually moved the blobs (see the
# header). Listing them while the objects are raw is what produced the
# phantom modifications above:
#   source-assets/**/*.glb            (42 MB of rigged source art)
#   source-assets/**/*.blend
#   docs/asset-inspections/**/*.png   (35 MB of records)
#   docs/asset-inspections/**/*.jpg
#   docs/ui-redesign/**/*.png
#   qa-evidence/**/*.png              (35 MB of records)
#   qa-evidence/**/*.jpg
#   exports/**/*.png                  (generated; exports/ is git-ignored)
